[[ja:官位相当制]]

The "{{ill|Kan'i sōtō system|lt=Equivalent Rank System|ja|官位相当制}}" was a system in Japan's Ritsuryo system.

People had ranks and they were assigned jobs based on their rank in the royal court

This is different from the Tang system which had people getting their ranks from their jobs

'''Kan'i Sōtōsei''' (官位相当制) refers to the bureaucratic ranking system in [[Japan]]'s [[Ritsuryō system]] that established a formal correspondence between [[rank (court rank)|ranks]] and [[government official|government posts]] assigned to officials.

== Overview ==
The Japanese Ritsuryō system's rank and office structure was codified under the {{ill|Taihō Ritsuryō|en|大宝律令}} and {{ill|Yōrō Ritsuryō|en|養老律令}}, specifically within the {{ill|Kan'i Ritsuryō|ja|官位令}}, modeled on China's {{ill|Tang dynasty|en|唐}} official grade system (官品令). However, there were fundamental differences between the two systems.

Under the Tang Ritsuryō system, official positions determined the bureaucratic hierarchy, with ranks (品階) serving as indicators of office grade. While officials were ideally assigned ranks corresponding to their offices, the limited availability of posts meant many officials held nominal offices or no post at all (散位). Consequently, participation in the bureaucratic hierarchy relied primarily on obtaining substantive posts, rendering ranks secondary to office.

In contrast, Japan's Ritsuryō system prioritized rank-based hierarchy, with offices assigned according to rank. Pre-Ritsuryō Japan had an existing clan-based hierarchy, necessitating a system that preserved traditional rankings while reorganizing clans into a bureaucratic framework. This need resulted in Japan's distinctive rank-first, office-second system, known as the Kan'i Sōtōsei.

Accordingly, the Kan'i Ritsuryō specified strict correspondence between ranks and offices. Officials advanced in rank first, gaining eligibility for corresponding offices only after achieving a higher rank. Unlike the Tang system, where office promotion dictated hierarchical advancement, Japan maintained rank as the principal measure of bureaucratic standing.

In formal titles, when rank and office were aligned, the office preceded the rank (e.g., ''Dewa no Kami, Jushii no Ge'' [出羽守従四位下]). Discrepancies in rank and office resulted in modified titles: if rank exceeded office, "Gyō" (行) was added before the office (e.g., ''Jushi no Ge Gyō Iyo no Kami'' [従四位下行伊予守]); if office exceeded rank, "Shu" (守) was added (e.g., ''Jugo no Ge Shu Ukyo no Daibu'' [従五位下守右京大夫]).

Over time, higher ranks became increasingly prevalent, while appointments to lower ranks (below [[Jōroku]] [正六位]) became rare as the Ritsuryō system declined.

